    "raw_text": "# Descriptive concept source excerpt\n\n[Source paragraph 1; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nHere the dots over the heat and work terms indicate the time rate of change of the variable. Proper thermodynamics terminology would require us to use the  $\\delta''$  instead of  $d$  in Equation 2.43 because of path dependency considerations, but for our purposes, we shall ignore this fact. The reader is advised to consult any thermodynamics text for a better understanding of the difference.\n\n[Source paragraph 2; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nThe substitutions that were performed to arrive at Equation 2.41 are not as straightforward in this case. Because we have material entering and leaving the CV, we can imagine that this material can enter or leave with a different pressure and density as it interacts with our fixed CV. Because of this, we must account for the energy used to make these changes. Alternatively, one can envision the material coming in at a higher pressure or density and wanting to push our imaginary CV outward, but since we fixed our CV, it cannot. The energy from this must go somewhere so it works on the fluid in and around our CV. Mathematically, this results in the energy term in Equation 2.44 having to include a  $pv$  term. This is sometimes known as flow work [10]. With this in mind, Equation 2.44 can be written as\n\n[Source paragraph 3; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nHere, by multiplying the intensive properties by the mass flow rate  $\\dot{m}$ , we have the rate of change of the energy terms. We have also arbitrarily assumed one inlet and one outlet. If more inlets or outlets in our CV were present and they had different mass flow rates or pressures, we would have to consider each with a term identical to our outlet or inlet terms given earlier. We now can make the substitution for our energy terms to yield\n\n[Source paragraph 4; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nIn this case, we have also assumed a uniform velocity over the inlets and outlets. With one inlet and outlet, the mass flow in must equal the mass flow out so we can write Equation 2.46 as\n\n[Source paragraph 5; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nAs a review, we have two equations that state the first law of thermodynamics. For a fixed mass of material (Lagrangian frame), we have\n\n[Source paragraph 6; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nand for a fixed volume that material can flow in and out of (Eulerian frame), we have\n\n[Source paragraph 7; 2.4 Thermodynamics; intervening text may be omitted]\n\nThese equations have been repeated here because of their critical importance to our work.\n\n",
